tent way to  showcase this so they all have to have  the ability to showcase a playground  they all have to have a demo app and so  that so even to do this that archetype  kind of comes into play where it's  really entangled into every kind of  piece and process that we have because  the archetype ensures that all the apps  have a demo it ensures that all the  applications can build the same they can  build in a way for this demo to work and  for them to show up on electrode  Explorer so that's why the archetype is  kind of this fundamental foundation for  us to move forward and so that's how we  went about solving discovery then we  gave this to product teams designers  that way everyone now has a library  fundamentally that gets built naturally  as you build components change  components and you can go back through  the versions of the components and  people now are on kind of the same page  of what do you have what can I use and  designers also have something to  leverage and what's really interesting  is if you have a product team that you  want to showcase some components that  you've built - all you do is send them  links to explore and they'll be able to  see it and play with it and validate if  it's you know if this is what they want  or not but then there's like certain  problems that we've that we've had and  that's around components breaking other  teams that's the worst thing about  having dependencies in the  usability you know the whole center  there's this reliance on other teams and  they're gonna break you and it's only a  matter of time you know that's gonna  happen and the only way that we can kind  of figure out how to solve this is  through C ICD and it actually there's it  puts a lot of you have to put a lot of  effort in building proper tests in your  application in the component so the  first pass of C ICD can be just like  component unit tests and make sure  that's solid but that doesn't guarantee  breakage because you change the  component and you make sure it works and  you probably test it on your application  and that works great but those other  apps you know who cares I got the  features to release and so there's no  real way to guarantee that so I think  the only the only way that we can think  of to really solve this problem is have  C ICD run automatically update other  applications and run their tests and run  their automation so if you push up a  surface n of PR and in the PR you can  specify is this is a new you know minor  upgrade or patch upgrade our CDC ICD  pipeline can you know wants to grab  other all other applications that use  that dependency automatically bump them  and run their automation suite and and  and kind of tell the person that's PR  and which ones are passing which ones  are failing that way before it gets  reviewed by the meta team and before it  gets accepted you can see what the  impact of that PR will do and what we  want to get to is we want to get to a  place where if things pass we want to  automatically upgrade apps that way  there's no there's no need for engineers  to even kind of go through and say oh  these things have been updated I know  they work and let me manually you know  bump up their versions but where it's  much more automated than that that way  if you will have a bug fix or some  feature improvement inherently all the  other applications will get it so CI  it's super critical it's even more  critical when it comes to archetypes  because if you release an archetype and  it breaks something you break every  single component an app and we've  unfortunately done that a few times  and we get yelled at really quickly so  you want to avoid really want to avoid  getting yelled at and get getting a like  a thousand things on slack with someone  broke me and it's the archetype because  it's like instant and it ruins your day  and so the CI CD is most important  during of checking archetype versions  making sure if you're trying to bump a  patch or minor of archetype you're not  breaking any component and you know any  app so there CI CD kind of scale in  terms of how many apps that has to run  at once for to do this efficiently grows  where you're gonna have to you know run  dozens of apps in parallel for every PR  that you do and be ready for that but  that's kind of the best machine  intensive a way that we can solve it  that's not human intensive and so the CI  CD rules they're critical you can't  really go about reusability with without  and I feel there's gonna be a lot of  very large cost and so then you'll have  you'll end up having the different teams  being able to use the different pieces  and now these teams ensure that they're  not going to break each other and so we  can't like have this so as you go about  let's say implementing the the ability  to reuse and in terms of technical  sharing and then you go about being able  to add archetypes you're going to manage  dependencies and people can discover  components and you have a team of people  that you can rely on to help ensure that  they're going you're going the right  path there's still a fundamental problem  that that kind of comes about and I call  that kind of the ecosystem of reuse and  when you want to take reusability on as  an organization what we found is that it  has to permeate every single layer of  kind of management and development  because it affects absolutely everything  when people when you're going to build  something you now have to not think of  you know what's specific to you but how  can other people leverage of this when  you want to create a component you're  gonna have a discussion we're gonna have  discussion with other individuals and  other organizations  about can this health can this help them  or not  and when you estimate you might have to  do estimate you're gonna make let's say  a dozen new components you're gonna make  a page you might lose a day to have the  proper discussions and the thinking  upfront and so this really permeates  every layer and it's a change of  philosophy so you can't say I'm gonna  have reuse but I'm gonna build things in  silos or i'm gonna have reuse and I'm  gonna build my features and I'm not  gonna put them inside the organization  for everyone else to use I'm just gonna  put them in my app because it's faster  immediately things fall apart so even  with all of this in place it's still  fragile and only I feel the only way to  make it robust as if if people feel the  same way have this thing important to  understand what they're trying to do and  why they're doing it so it's really a  philosophy change and and how you build  moving forward and then there are still  challenges that we have not particularly  you know have really good solves for for  example when there's new libraries or  new technologies that are coming out  that you want to leverage and you want  to test out this is gonna work is it not  the benefits of the pros with everyone  we're using the same thing with everyone  in the same common platform how are you  able to innovate how are you able to get  teams to really try something new versus  not and so there are some challenges the  the fragility of if teams are not under  the same philosophy and and and what if  they're in a certain team people that  really want to experiment with a new  build system and if they go down that  route everything that they build cannot  be reused and they don't have to rebuild  everything from scratch but these are  kind of the challenges that you face and  that you have to kind of talk through  and and figure out a process and how to  do it and so that's kind of the way in  which we have been doing reuse and I  think we have five hundred something  components across 50 apps and a few  hundred engineers  we currently have three tenants that are  on kind of the electoral platform we  have  my grocery and a piece of Sam's Club and  where they're leveraging this reuse and  I think we have a decent system down  there's obviously room for us to really  help kind of continue the the philosophy  and pushing that forward improve our CI  CD some more but I think that this  general concept works thank you very  much  [Applause]
 

